#clinical-finding(title: [Autism and ADHD as Differential-Diagnostic Confounders for ME/CFS])[
  The presentations of autistic and ADHD patients overlap with ME/CFS across fatigue, sleep
  disturbance, social withdrawal, and cognitive slowing, creating diagnostic confusion. In a Dutch
  register survey of autistic adults (n=1,211), 24.6% reported at least one prior psychiatric
  diagnosis perceived as a misdiagnosis; chronic fatigue syndrome / burnout-related disorders were
  among the listed categories, judged a misdiagnosis by 4% of the total sample
  @Kentrou2024autisticMisdiagnosis. That pattern shows energy-related diagnoses figure in the
  diagnostic confusion autistic adults experience before autism recognition; it does not mean
  clinicians dismiss ME/CFS as "just autism." Separately, adults with ADHD and chronic fatigue
  syndrome show overlapping psychological profiles (functional impairment, mood disturbance, lower
  self-efficacy) @Rogers2017ADHDFatigue. The differential discriminator remains PEM: exertion-triggered
  delayed multi-day worsening points to ME/CFS regardless of whether ADHD or autism is present.

  *Clinical implication:* ADHD and autism are not alternative diagnoses for ME/CFS; they are
  comorbidities that can coexist. The psychological consequences of this overlap are discussed in
  Section @subsec:social-emotional-dysfunction.
  *Certainty: 0.55.* (Observational survey + comparative cohort; no ME/CFS-criteria validation in
  the autistic sample; retrospective self-report.)
] <cf:neurodivergent-differential-diagnosis>
